 | `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_MMVQ` | **OFF when unset.** Only an exact `1` admits `GGML_TYPE_SX8` in `ggml_vk_should_use_mmvq()`; unset and every other value leave every width on the f32 dequant mat-vec. Added 2026-09-10 (lane-230/R46 commit C). | Scope: the `q8_1` integer-dot `mul_mat_vec` pipeline for `GGML_TYPE_SX8` and nothing else. The pipeline is created unconditionally beside the `Q8_0` row and the `b_type == Q8_1` selector switch lists the type; this env var is the only thing that makes `quantize_y` true, so with it unset the pipeline is compiled and never dispatched. The `_id` mirror is deliberately NOT admitted (declared with an `ARIFI-SYNC-SET ... EXCEPT SX8=` marker): no id `q8_1` pipeline is generated for S-X8, so admitting it there would select a null pipeline. | **Only on the 248320x5120 embed/output shape, and only while investigating.** That is where B2's `n <= 3` whole-block gate hands wide batches back to the per-position kernel and this path is worth +38 to +97%. Do not enable it for a general serve: the FFN shapes lose. | **MEASURED, MIXED, so it ships OFF.** RIG-A, `test-backend-ops`, 3 interleaved rounds per arm in one quiet hold, base = the unchanged kernel, candidate = commit C on top of the gated commit B2 (`32-paired-c.txt`, 96 cells, raw timings in `32-paired-c.json`). **Against the SHIPPED kernel it loses at `n=7` on three of four primary 27B shapes** - `-28.37%` (4096x14336), `-26.13%` (17408x5120), `-13.29%` (5120x17408) - **raw rounds non-overlapping**, so repeatable, not noise. The PREREG promotion bar (>5% median at `n=1..4`, no >3% repeatable regression on any primary shape) is also missed at `n=4`, 4096x14336: `-6.85%` against B2 (DERIVED, cross-session). **Where it wins it wins large**: 248320x5120 `+38.00 / +45.73 / +46.43 / +51.79%` at `n=1..4` against the unchanged kernel, `+94.85%` at `n=6`. A per-width admit was REFUSED as overfitting - the winning region is one shape at two widths. The `n=7` cliff is **non-monotonic** (`+13.69 / -13.88 / +17.68%` at `n=6/7/8` on 5120x17408 against B2), which points at a `NUM_COLS == 7` tail defect in the mmvq framework rather than a tuning loss; that is where a narrowed admit must start. Routing was proven by a **planted-RED in commit C's dot product only**: with the env unset every `sx8` MUL_MAT case is green, with `=1` ten of twelve go red and the two `mul_mm` cases stay green. Evidence: `research/local-inference/lane-evidence/2026-09-10-lane-230-r46-sx8-mmv/`. |
 | `GGML_VK_SX8_MMV_ROWS` / `GGML_VK_SX8_MMV_WG` | **unset = the probed default** (workgroup = the device subgroup width when it is a multiple of 32, else 32; rows = 2). Read once at pipeline creation. Added 2026-09-11 (lane-232/R47a), extended 2026-09-11 (lane-230/R46b commit D). | Scope: every S-X8 `mul_mat_vec` pipeline, f32/f16/q8_1/id alike. An override is taken WHOLE or refused WHOLE against this device's real limits: workgroup a positive multiple of 32, within `maxComputeWorkGroupInvocations`, and `NUM_COLS * rows * wg * 4` within `maxComputeSharedMemorySize` at the WIDEST `NUM_COLS`. **Setting either variable also switches the R46b shape-tuned lookup OFF for the whole run** — a run that kept the measured table on the cells the arm does not name would be a mixed arm. A stderr receipt names APPLIED or REFUSED once per distinct pair. | Sweep instrument. Use it to pair rows 1/2/4/8 against workgroups 64/128/256 out of one binary, or to pin the pre-R46b behaviour for an A/B. Not a serve setting. | On the 780M (subgroup 64, 32 KiB shared) `rows=8/wg=256` is REFUSED by shared memory and the run keeps the default; `rows=4/wg=256` and `rows=8/wg=128` sit exactly on the 32,768-byte limit, are admitted, and measured 12/12 GREEN on `MUL_MAT` (`research/local-inference/lane-evidence/2026-09-11-lane-232-r47a-alloc-instrument/33-boundary-rows4-wg256.txt`, `34-boundary-rows8-wg128.txt`). |
 | S-X8 mat-vec shape tuning (R47d lookup) | **Code, not an environment variable. ON by device probe** — subgroup width 64 with `maxComputeSharedMemorySize == 32768`, the SUBGROUP workgroup class, f32 activations, and only for the exact (m, k, n) cells the table names. Added 2026-09-11 (lane-230/R46b commit D). | Scope: `GGML_TYPE_SX8` `mul_mat_vec` with f32 activations. 31 measured cells over the four real 27B S-X8 decode shapes at n=1..8; the n=1 FFN orientations take rows=4/wg=256 and the 248320x5120 output takes rows=8/wg=64, which is why one global pair cannot express it. Every other shape, every n>8, the f16/q8_1/id paths and every other device keep the probed default. A stderr receipt at pipeline creation names ON/OFF and the probed default. | Nothing to operate. To measure against it, set `GGML_VK_SX8_MMV_ROWS`/`_WG` (which turns the lookup off whole). | **MEASURED on lane-232's base, which does NOT carry R46's `n <= 3` whole-block decode.** Every selected cell read >= 0.97 of the probed default in BOTH rounds, best cells +12.18% (248320x5120 n=2) and +10.76% (n=6); source `research/local-inference/lane-evidence/2026-09-11-lane-232-r47a-alloc-instrument/R47D-TRACES-SWEEP.md`, "Sweep selection". R46 replaces the decode math and row-blocking loop at `n <= 3` and leaves the reduction shape untouched, so the shared-memory arithmetic still holds, but **the n=1/2/3 optima are UNVERIFIED on the rebased kernel** until they are re-measured on the R46b stage. No held-out deployment guarantee is claimed. |
+| `GGML_VK_PLACEMENT=bulk-large-heap` | **OFF when unset; unset changes no byte of the existing placement chain.** Only the exact string `bulk-large-heap` is accepted. Added 2026-09-11 (lane-230/R46b commit E). | Scope: the bulk device buffers the model loader suballocates weights from (`ggml_backend_vk_buffer_type_alloc_buffer`), and nothing else — prealloc scratch and staging keep their current paths. The policy asks for a `DEVICE_LOCAL` type on the LARGEST heap and excludes every other heap for those buffers; grouping is untouched. A refusal is not fatal: that one buffer falls back to the stock chain and says so on stderr. A receipt names the chosen heap **with tracing off**. | Only to test the R47d placement hypothesis, in proof cells A/B, against an unchanged-policy control. | **UNMEASURED as a fix — commissioned, not proven.** R47d established the mechanism it targets: the placement chain tests each allocation against the WHOLE heap size with no cumulative accounting (`ggml-vulkan.cpp` `ggml_vk_find_memory_properties`), so `GGML_VK_PREFER_HOST_MEMORY=1` puts bulk weights on host-visible heap 1 and a 256 MiB grouping puts them in the small combined-property heap 2, and the first pinned upload then dies at `submit seq=0` with `ErrorUnknown` in both arms (`R47D-TRACES-SWEEP.md`, "Exact failure boundaries"). The driver-internal cause of `ErrorUnknown` is **UNSCORED**. The cumulative requirement reservation with rollback that the same commission asks for is **NOT implemented**; this is heap selection only. |
+| `GGML_VK_ALLOC_TRACE=1` | **OFF when unset, zero cost.** Added 2026-09-11 (lane-232/R47a). | Scope: diagnostic only. Prints requirement bytes, `memoryTypeBits`, every candidate memory type/heap tried, the chosen type and heap, per-heap live bytes, the driver's `VK_EXT_memory_budget` figures (or `budget=unavailable`, never fabricated zeros), and one line per queue submit with its copy batch and `VkResult`. | Load-failure and placement investigation. Never in a timed run: the trace is large (10.6 MB for one 27B load) and writes on the hot path. | Diagnostic instrument, no performance claim. It is what produced the two exact failure boundaries in `R47D-TRACES-SWEEP.md`. |
 | `--spec-draft-adaptive` | **OFF.** CLI flag; sizes each draft from a per-sequence EMA of accepted tokens instead of a fixed `--spec-draft-n-max`, which becomes a safety ceiling rather than a tuning parameter. Backing off is averaged (alpha 0.25, ~4-step memory); probing upward is additive. Reset on `begin()` — a new prompt or a reused server slot — never mid-generation. | Scope: `common/speculative.cpp` draft-length selection. Sits in the `else` of this fork's existing DFlash2 adaptive selector, so a controller already armed keeps precedence; the MTP sites keep their own adaptive precedence. Speculation stays distribution-preserving, so the flag can move throughput and **cannot** move output. | Turn it on where content type varies within a session and no single fixed depth is right — its author's motivating measurement is that the correct depth is *opposite* for prose and for JSON. Sweep it against your own fixed depth before adopting. | **UNMEASURED ON RIG-A — no A/B for this flag exists on this hardware, and none should be quoted.** The measured table in the source commit was taken on a **Radeon 8060S** by the upstream author (three drafter configurations × prose/json × fixed-vs-adaptive, 300 greedy tokens on a Qwen3.8-27B ROCmFP4-FAST target). Those are **upstream-reported numbers for a different GPU** and are deliberately not restated here as results of this fork. Default OFF for that reason alone: the mechanism is sound and the evidence for it is somebody else's box. |
 | `GGML_ARIFI_FA_DEQUANT_KV` | **Device default when unset, which is ON on every device** (upstream behaviour). `1` forces the f16 dequant-KV scratch copy ON, `0` forces the in-place quantized-KV read. Added 2026-09-06 (lane-212/R19), default flipped to ON 2026-09-07 (lane-220/R27). | Scope: Vulkan flash attention with a quantized K and V, prefill only (`neq1 >= 64`); decode never reaches it. One stderr receipt line `ggml_vulkan: FA dequant-KV scratch: ON\|OFF (device default\|GGML_ARIFI_FA_DEQUANT_KV)` binds every A/B log to the path it ran. Bit-identical either way - the same FA math with f32 accumulation, only the KV read strategy differs. A device that measures the scratch copy as a cost flips its own default at `fa_deq_device_default`, with its number. | Leave it unset. Set `0` to reproduce the pre-R27 AMD RDNA3 arm on the same binary, or on a device where the scratch copy is measurably a cost. | **The knob and the receipt are the result; the magnitude is NOT.** R19D measured `+8.08%` clean prefill on one binary and R27 flipped the default on it. The R18 wave recomputed the same banked rounds on 2026-09-08 and **it does not reproduce**: gate OFF vs ON on one binary reads decode `+0.49%` CI95 `[-1.62%, +2.59%]` and prefill `+0.85%` `[+0.36%, +1.33%]`; the DFlash2 arm reads decode `-0.91%` `[-1.93%, +0.12%]`. Do not quote `+8.08%`. Evidence: `research/local-inference/lane-evidence/2026-09-02-lane-209/r18-kernel-wave/R18-KERNEL-WAVE-REPORT.md`, `.../r19-seat-prefill/R19D-ENGAGEMENT-LEGS.md`. |
 | `LLAMA_DFLASH_FUSED_INJECT` | **OFF when unset on this fork - the LEGACY encode+decode path. This DIVERGES from upstream 662a0b012, deliberately.** `1` selects the fused injection (upstream shape). Added 2026-09-06 (lane-212/R18), default set to legacy 2026-09-08 (lane-225/R38). The C API default (`llama_cparams::dflash_fused_inject`) stays **fused**, because the legacy shape is a caller contract - the caller must `llama_encode` the chunk first - so only `common/speculative.cpp`, which owns both halves, defaults it off. | Scope: the DFlash2 drafter's K/V injection only. Fused = one `llama_decode` per chunk with the encoder (fc + rms norm) in-graph. Legacy = `llama_encode` the chunk through `graph<true>`, read the encoded rows back through `llama_get_embeddings_nextn`, feed them to the injection decode at the draft width. Nothing removed: `graph<true>`, the fused embd branch and the legacy host path all stay compiled. One receipt line per process names the arm. | Set `1` to restore upstream behaviour, and on any box that has not measured this pair. The default here is a **conservative choice on a sub-floor number, not a measured win** - see the cost cell before treating it as a recommendation. | **DOCUMENTED-TAKE, no gain, and the default flip is under the floor.** Seat DFlash2 decode `-0.98%` CI95 `[-1.16%, -0.80%]` fused vs legacy; U-IQ4XS `+0.05%` `[-0.56%, +0.67%]`; U-Q3KXL `-0.87%` `[-2.50%, +0.77%]`. Against this box's own build floor - the R18 wave's INERT controls, where both arms ran identical code, moved decode by up to **+3.71%** and prefill by up to **-8.28%**, both excluding zero, so **none of these three clears it** and the direction is all the flip rests on. Quality: F-141 both arms on the seat, `test-quantize-fns` identical to the bank. Evidence: `.../r18-kernel-wave/r15c2-dflash-encoder-fusion`. |
diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp b/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp
index 127005493..423b9e372 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp
@@ -3769,11 +3769,48 @@ static void ggml_vk_queue_command_pools_cleanup(vk_device& device) {
     }
 }
 
-static std::vector<uint32_t> ggml_vk_find_memory_properties(const vk::PhysicalDeviceMemoryProperties* mem_props, vk::MemoryRequirements* mem_req, vk::MemoryPropertyFlags flags) {
+// lane-230 / R46b commit E: GGML_VK_PLACEMENT=bulk-large-heap, an OPT-IN explicit placement
+// policy. Unset keeps every existing selection byte-for-byte.
+//
+// R47d traced both load failures to the same shape: the placement chain accepts a memory type
+// whenever the WHOLE heap is at least as large as this one allocation (no cumulative accounting),
+// so GGML_VK_PREFER_HOST_MEMORY=1 puts the bulk weights on the host-visible heap 1 and a 256 MiB
+// grouping puts them in the small combined-property heap 2, and the FIRST pinned upload then dies
+// at submit seq=0 with ErrorUnknown. The policy routes bulk weight buffers to a DEVICE_LOCAL type
+// on the LARGEST heap and excludes every other heap for them; grouping is untouched, and staging
+// and scratch keep the paths they already use. Receipt:
+// research/local-inference/lane-evidence/2026-09-11-lane-232-r47a-alloc-instrument/
+// R47D-TRACES-SWEEP.md, "Placement levers and loader commission".
+//
+// NOT implemented here and OWED: the thread-safe cumulative requirement reservation with rollback
+// that the same commission asks for. This policy is heap SELECTION only.
+static bool ggml_vk_placement_bulk_large_heap() {
+    static const bool on = [] {
+        const char * s = getenv("GGML_VK_PLACEMENT");
+        return s != nullptr && strcmp(s, "bulk-large-heap") == 0;
+    }();
+    return on;
+}
+
+static uint32_t ggml_vk_largest_heap(const vk::PhysicalDeviceMemoryProperties & mem_props) {
+    uint32_t best = 0;
+    for (uint32_t h = 1; h < mem_props.memoryHeapCount; ++h) {
+        if (mem_props.memoryHeaps[h].size > mem_props.memoryHeaps[best].size) {
+            best = h;
+        }
+    }
+    return best;
+}
+
+// only_heap != UINT32_MAX restricts the candidate list to that one heap (R46b commit E).
+static std::vector<uint32_t> ggml_vk_find_memory_properties(const vk::PhysicalDeviceMemoryProperties* mem_props, vk::MemoryRequirements* mem_req, vk::MemoryPropertyFlags flags, uint32_t only_heap = UINT32_MAX) {
     std::vector<uint32_t> indices;
 
     for (uint32_t i = 0; i < mem_props->memoryTypeCount; ++i) {
         vk::MemoryType memory_type = mem_props->memoryTypes[i];
+        if (only_heap != UINT32_MAX && memory_type.heapIndex != only_heap) {
+            continue;
+        }
         if ((mem_req->memoryTypeBits & ((uint64_t)1 << i)) &&
             (flags & memory_type.propertyFlags) == flags &&
             mem_props->memoryHeaps[memory_type.heapIndex].size >= mem_req->size) {
@@ -3973,7 +4010,7 @@ static void vk_alloc_trace_record_copy(vk_context & subctx, const char * tag, co
 }
 
 static vk_buffer ggml_vk_create_buffer(vk_device& device, size_t size, const std::initializer_list<vk::MemoryPropertyFlags> & req_flags_list,
-                                       void *import_ptr = nullptr) {
+                                       void *import_ptr = nullptr, uint32_t only_heap = UINT32_MAX) {
     VK_LOG_DEBUG("ggml_vk_create_buffer(" << device->name << ", " << size << ", " << to_string(req_flags_list.begin()[0]) << ", " << to_string(req_flags_list.begin()[req_flags_list.size()-1]) << ")");
     if (size > device->max_buffer_size) {
         throw vk::OutOfDeviceMemoryError("Requested buffer size exceeds device buffer size limit");
@@ -4095,7 +4132,7 @@ static vk_buffer ggml_vk_create_buffer(vk_device& device, size_t size, const std
         for (auto it = req_flags_list.begin(); it != req_flags_list.end(); it++) {
             const auto & req_flags = *it;
 
-            const std::vector<uint32_t> memory_type_indices = ggml_vk_find_memory_properties(&mem_props, &mem_req, req_flags);
+            const std::vector<uint32_t> memory_type_indices = ggml_vk_find_memory_properties(&mem_props, &mem_req, req_flags, only_heap);
 
             if (memory_type_indices.empty()) {
                 if (alloc_trace) {
@@ -4189,9 +4226,40 @@ static vk_buffer ggml_vk_create_buffer_check(vk_device& device, size_t size, vk:
     }
 }
 
-static vk_buffer ggml_vk_create_buffer_device(vk_device& device, size_t size) {
+static vk_buffer ggml_vk_create_buffer_device(vk_device& device, size_t size, bool bulk = false) {
     vk_buffer buf;
     try {
+        // lane-230 / R46b commit E: bulk weight buffers, policy ON. DEVICE_LOCAL on the largest
+        // heap and nothing else. A failure here is NOT fatal: fall through to the stock chain
+        // below, because a policy that turns a working load into a hard failure is worse than the
+        // placement it is trying to fix.
+        if (bulk && ggml_vk_placement_bulk_large_heap()) {
+            const vk::PhysicalDeviceMemoryProperties mem_props = device->physical_device.getMemoryProperties();
+            const uint32_t heap = ggml_vk_largest_heap(mem_props);
+            // stderr, not GGML_LOG_INFO, and NOT gated on GGML_VK_ALLOC_TRACE: the paired
+            // performance cells for this policy run with tracing off and still have to prove which
+            // heap the run used.
+            static std::mutex receipt_mutex;
+            static std::set<uint32_t> receipted;
+            bool first = false;
+            {
+                std::lock_guard<std::mutex> guard(receipt_mutex);
+                first = receipted.insert(heap).second;
+            }
+            if (first) {
+                fprintf(stderr, "ggml_vulkan: placement policy bulk-large-heap: bulk weights -> heap %u "
+                                "(size=%llu B, DEVICE_LOCAL only, every other heap excluded)\n",
+                        heap, (unsigned long long) mem_props.memoryHeaps[heap].size);
+            }
+            try {
+                return ggml_vk_create_buffer(device, size, {vk::MemoryPropertyFlagBits::eDeviceLocal}, nullptr, heap);
+            } catch (const vk::SystemError& e) {
+                fprintf(stderr, "ggml_vulkan: placement policy bulk-large-heap: heap %u refused %llu B (%s); "
+                                "falling back to the default placement chain for this buffer\n",
+                        heap, (unsigned long long) size, e.what());
+            }
+        }
+
         if (device->prefer_host_memory) {
             buf = ggml_vk_create_buffer(device, size, {vk::MemoryPropertyFlagBits::eHostVisible | vk::MemoryPropertyFlagBits::eHostCoherent,
                                                        vk::MemoryPropertyFlagBits::eDeviceLocal});
@@ -18475,7 +18543,9 @@ static ggml_backend_buffer_t ggml_backend_vk_buffer_type_alloc_buffer(ggml_backe
 
     vk_buffer dev_buffer = nullptr;
     try {
-        dev_buffer = ggml_vk_create_buffer_device(ctx->device, size);
+        // R46b commit E: this is the BULK path - the grouped weight/tensor blocks the loader
+        // suballocates from. The prealloc scratch buffers deliberately do not carry the flag.
+        dev_buffer = ggml_vk_create_buffer_device(ctx->device, size, /* bulk */ true);
     } catch (const vk::SystemError& e) {
         return nullptr;
     }
